#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059668 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059434 :question/id #uuid "2138def0-a28a-5018-b93f-c63022825ecb" :question/math-academy-id "e-13411" :question/problem "The point $(a,b)$ lies on the curve $y=x^4.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y = (x-2)^4-6?$ \n\n- $(a-2, b-6)$\n\n- $(a+2,b+ 6)$\n\n- $(a+2, b-6)$\n\n- $(a-2,b+ 6)$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y = (x-2)^4-6,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y=x^4.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $2$ units to the right and get the graph of $y=(x-2)^4.$ After this transformation, the point $(a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+2,b).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $6$ units down and obtain the graph of $y = (x-2)^4-6.$ After this transformation, the point $(a+2,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+2,b-6).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe are told that the point $(a, b)$ lies on the graph of $y = x^4$, which means:\n\n$$\nb=a^4\n$$\n\nNow, we want to find which point must lie on the graph of:\n\n$$\ny = (x-2)^4-6\n$$\n\nLet’s test what happens when we substitute $x = a + 2$ into this new equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(a+2-2{)}^{4}-6 \\\\  & ={a}^{4}-6 \\\\  & =b-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, when $x = a + 2$, we get $y = b - 6.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bd940e7-fccc-5811-b07a-11e4843b1785" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048753 :topic/math-academy-id 2841 :topic/title "Combining Reflections With Other Graph Transformations"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060003 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cf13dd1-d493-55fb-94ad-c50574dc8bb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a,b+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253778} {:answer/id #uuid "71186c58-32dc-5b95-a447-4b4569b59c09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a,b+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254672} {:answer/id #uuid "a275b10b-0be9-59e6-8c33-ce2b9dcd6958" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a+1,b+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255104} {:answer/id #uuid "b66ae15a-a2f1-5d79-b5f4-12bbbae7e98e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a-1,b+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255286} {:answer/id #uuid "e6b76ad7-f3fe-559a-8617-6ec58d9ce1a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a+2,b+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255701}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a275b10b-0be9-59e6-8c33-ce2b9dcd6958" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a+1,b+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255104} :answer-field/id #uuid "11178a13-a5c1-5fd6-b0f8-608b3946af04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255943}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "933f2dda-2343-544a-8ef3-2160dd14e9e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-121220" :question/problem "The point $(a,b)$ lies on the curve $y={x}^{2}.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=(x-1{)}^{2}+2?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=(x-1{)}^{2}+1,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y={x}^{2}.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $1$ unit to the right and get the graph of $y=(x-1{)}^{2}.$ After this transformation, the point $(a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+1,b).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $2$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=(x-1{)}^{2}+2.$ After this transformation, the point $(a+1,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+1,b+2).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(a,b)$ lies on the graph of $y={x}^{2}$, we have that ${a}^{2}=b.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=(x-1{)}^{2}+2.$ Substituting $x=a+1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(a+1-1{)}^{2}+2 \\\\  & ={a}^{2}+2 \\\\  & =b+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(a+1,b+2)$ must lie on $y=(x-1{)}^{2}+2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060258 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "215b733e-02f5-559d-b497-8039bd0abddb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290773} {:answer/id #uuid "40347c00-b783-5421-939f-ab3ef7825eb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290900} {:answer/id #uuid "5451ee39-3132-55be-b496-cccbb1ecfcd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290991} {:answer/id #uuid "669f54dd-330e-5187-a457-8ec777fa2db4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291068} {:answer/id #uuid "832359a3-815f-50ef-8abf-011a2185b994" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291186}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "669f54dd-330e-5187-a457-8ec777fa2db4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291068} :answer-field/id #uuid "7865af2d-939b-59a2-9e75-cdf7738a3599" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292113}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e4d7f8b8-ba0a-5db0-954f-1969ddfefa81" :question/math-academy-id "q-26398" :question/problem "The point $(1,4)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=f(x-1)+1?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=f(x-1)+1,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, shift the graph horizontally by $1$ unit to the right and get the graph of \n$y=f(x-1).$  After this transformation, the point $(1,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1+1,4)=(2,4).\n$$\n\n- Finally,  we shift the result vertically by $1$  unit up and obtain the graph of\n$y=f(x-1)+1.$ After this transformation, the point $(2,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2,4+1)=(2,5).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(1,4)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$, we have that $f(1)=4.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=f(x-1)+1.$ Substituting $x=2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =f(2-1)+1 \\\\  & =f(1)+1 \\\\  & =4+1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(2,5)$ must lie on $y=f(x-1)+1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233288 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "00e41b54-1b06-55e4-ba21-6bbf8797df49" :question/math-academy-id "q-121381" :question/problem "The point $(a,b)$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{x}.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=\\sqrt{x-1}-1?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=\\sqrt{x-1}-1,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $1$ unit to the right and get the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x-1}.$ After this transformation, the point $(a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+1,b).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $1$ unit down and obtain the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x-1}-1.$ After this transformation, the point $(a+1,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+1,b-1).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(a,b)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x}$, we have that $\\sqrt{a}=b.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x-1}-1.$ Substituting $x=a+1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{a+1-1}-1 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{a}-1 \\\\  & =b-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(a+1,b-1)$ must lie on $y=\\sqrt{x-1}-1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233289 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7d1f17ba-35cc-58b5-9aa2-08d577267c2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-121393" :question/problem "The point $(a,b)$ lies on the curve $y={x}^{3}.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=2(x+2)^{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=2(x+2)^{3},$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y={x}^{3}.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $2$ units to the left and get the graph of $y=(x+2)^{3}.$ After this transformation, the point $(a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a-2,b).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we stretch the graph by a scale factor of  $2$ parallel to the $y$ -axis and get the graph of  $y=2(x+2)^{3}.$ After this transformation, the point $(a-2,b)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(a-2,2b).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(a,b)$ lies on the graph of $y={x}^{3}$, we have that ${a}^{3}=b.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=2(x+2)^{3}.$ Substituting $x=a-2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2(a-2+2)^{3} \\\\  & =2{a}^{3} \\\\  & =2b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(a-2,2b)$ must lie on $y=2(x+2)^{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233534 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4a80933c-10f7-51d8-9b1a-039e3550e2d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-158540" :question/problem "The point $(-1,1)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=3f(x+2)?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=3f(x+2),$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, shift the graph horizontally by $2$ unit to the left and get the graph of \n$y=f(x+2).$  After this transformation, the point $(-1,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1-2,1)=(-3,1).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we stretch the result by a scale factor of $3$ parallel to the y-axis and get the graph of \n$y=3f(x+2).$ After this transformation, the point $(-3,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-3,3\\cdot 1)=(-3,3).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(-1,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$, we have that $f(-1)=1.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=3f(x+2).$ Substituting $x=-3$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3f(-3+2) \\\\  & =3f(-1) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-3,3)$ must lie on $y=3f(x+2).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256539 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "165e3ec5-5bc9-5cba-9c8a-6b6ebe75a58b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a-2,b+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253863} {:answer/id #uuid "5828d10b-52d2-519e-94d5-63d2d5b4c550" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a-2,b-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254445} {:answer/id #uuid "74fc90a5-c321-5fe2-a988-759338bbb191" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a-2,3b)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254707} {:answer/id #uuid "b59d2443-c7bf-54dc-9108-20e2c63d903b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a+2,b+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255271} {:answer/id #uuid "c709185f-e827-59eb-bd52-699c51a7e1b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a+2,b-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255448}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b59d2443-c7bf-54dc-9108-20e2c63d903b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a+2,b+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255271} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca534256-c181-5ac0-b56d-2d5a591a2172" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256283}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9b584cb7-ff23-5cf8-a53c-0371ad6cd2e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-121383" :question/problem "The point $(a,b)$ lies on the curve $y={2}^{x}.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y={2}^{x-2}+3?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y={2}^{x-2}+3,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y={2}^{x}.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $2$ units to the right and get the graph of $y={2}^{x-2}.$ After this transformation, the point $(a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+2,b).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $3$ units up and obtain the graph of $y={2}^{x-2}+3.$ After this transformation, the point $(a+2,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+2,b+3).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(a,b)$ lies on the graph of $y={2}^{x}$, we have that ${2}^{a}=b.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y={2}^{x-2}+3.$ Substituting $x=a+2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={2}^{a+2-2}+3 \\\\  & ={2}^{a}+3 \\\\  & =b+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(a+2,b+3)$ must lie on $y={2}^{x-2}+3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256540 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44c21c55-17bb-5c41-88d1-6c299c881b29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a-2,b-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254253} {:answer/id #uuid "80df87fd-c227-5716-9dc2-6b062371184a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a-3,b-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254808} {:answer/id #uuid "b5f3a22f-dbdf-598f-a34c-1777eda4f583" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a+3,b-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255276} {:answer/id #uuid "bbff3184-f777-51ba-8744-de29245ae71f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a+3,b+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255349} {:answer/id #uuid "f87700c2-b05d-5438-9cf7-184870d44569" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a-3,b+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80df87fd-c227-5716-9dc2-6b062371184a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a-3,b-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254808} :answer-field/id #uuid "074aa316-c74e-53ab-a8c8-8f76eb00e9c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255928}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "19d2f443-e38d-5ded-a277-4288cfd36f31" :question/math-academy-id "q-121385" :question/problem "The point $(a,b)$ lies on the curve $y=|x|.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=|x+3|-2?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=|x+3|-2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y=|x|.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $3$ units to the left and get the graph of $y=|x+3|.$ After this transformation, the point $(a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a-3,b).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $2$ units down and obtain the graph of $y=|x+3|-2.$ After this transformation, the point $(a-3,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a-3,b-2).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(a,b)$ lies on the graph of $y=|x|$, we have that $|a|=b.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=|x+3|-2.$ Substituting $x=a-3$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =|a-3+3|-2 \\\\  & =|a|-2 \\\\  & =b-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(a-3,b-2)$ must lie on $y=|x+3|-2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256541 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e5bc30c-2abb-52a7-bb51-cb184ad226c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{a}{2},b-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254343} {:answer/id #uuid "6cad43fd-a241-5e56-82fc-2eeac4a6bb5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a+2,b+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254635} {:answer/id #uuid "81df30f7-1414-57bb-a6ef-282731df4980" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({a}^{3/2},b+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254821} {:answer/id #uuid "adde4716-7e5d-5b0d-b0ca-454d703c35b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2a,b+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255204} {:answer/id #uuid "f6019e36-937d-5523-941c-72dc4b368337" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{a}{2},b+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255822}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "adde4716-7e5d-5b0d-b0ca-454d703c35b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2a,b+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255204} :answer-field/id #uuid "4647e489-d352-5af5-8c97-6318e3c26f3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256054}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d40ea2d8-4f8c-52c6-b88c-21d92eb5a3d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-121399" :question/problem "The point $(a,b)$ lies on the curve $y={3}^{x}.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y={3}^{x/2}+1?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y={3}^{x/2}+1,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y={3}^{x}.$\n\n- Then, we stretch the graph by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $x$-axis and get the graph of  $y={3}^{x/2}.$ After this transformation, the point $(a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2a,b).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $1$ unit up and obtain the graph of $y={3}^{x/2}+1.$ After this transformation, the point $(2a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2a,b+1).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(a,b)$ lies on the graph of $y={3}^{x}$, we have that ${3}^{a}=b.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y={3}^{x/2}+1.$ Substituting $x=2a$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={3}^{(2a)/2}+1 \\\\  & ={3}^{a}+1 \\\\  & =b+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(2a,b+1)$ must lie on $y={3}^{x/2}+1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292672 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33c00c15-b13b-5bcc-8be2-21e93f4c417e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290839} {:answer/id #uuid "487a2755-1c9b-50dc-a9ef-825d319c08d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290930} {:answer/id #uuid "5a5b7f1c-6ca6-519d-9324-afd97504caa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291021} {:answer/id #uuid "bfbeb462-5ad4-5751-9859-039c2c20e85a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291457} {:answer/id #uuid "f4debbbb-a978-5aa2-807b-d09d5e6771d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291662}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33c00c15-b13b-5bcc-8be2-21e93f4c417e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290839} :answer-field/id #uuid "337977c2-17f5-5416-91d9-a9bbf15f1e4e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291907}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6eb852b1-3670-5b7e-89d9-1b29c405f01a" :question/math-academy-id "q-26594" :question/problem "The point  $(3,1)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)-1?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=-f(x)-1,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the graph across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(3,-(1))=(3,-1).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $1$ unit down and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)-1.$ After this transformation, the point $(3,-1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(3,-1-1)=(3,-2).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(3,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$, we have that $f(3)=1.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)-1.$ Substituting $x=3$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(3)-1 \\\\  & =-(1)-1 \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(3,-2)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)-1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517145 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48e277a6-1836-5a2a-995b-c9a72a4bc82d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(8,-15)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517149} {:answer/id #uuid "78cbfab6-ec0f-5d95-9353-c44c732e93f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-8,15)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517157} {:answer/id #uuid "a0e09e1c-59d6-50c2-9163-d20b2885a669" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-8,-15)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517160} {:answer/id #uuid "dab93924-a3f7-5d1c-ba43-dff9a609619c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-8,-22)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517164} {:answer/id #uuid "e24594ac-0835-5d34-a8a7-19a8574c8cbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(8,-22)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517165}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0e09e1c-59d6-50c2-9163-d20b2885a669" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-8,-15)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517160} :answer-field/id #uuid "abeeca41-84fa-55a4-ba65-c8230425d31b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517162}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3d089253-9314-5487-9a48-4dddc95358e7" :question/problem "The point $(-8,8)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)-7?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=-f(x)-7,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the graph across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-8,8)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-8,-8).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $7$ units down and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)-7.$ After this transformation, the point $(-8,-8)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-8,-15).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(-8,8)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$, we have that $f(-8)=8.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)-7.$ Substituting $x=-8$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &=-f(-8)-7 \\\\ &=-( 8 )-7 \\\\ &=-8-7 \\\\ &=-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-8,-15)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)-7.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517154 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d506d7f-07cd-59ac-a403-64c70b810b0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{a}{2},b+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517146} {:answer/id #uuid "65b532c4-aaff-57a9-bb71-1e14f28d0636" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a+2,b-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517153} {:answer/id #uuid "6f955108-7f48-51fc-aaaa-ed9327143578" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{a}{2},b-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517156} {:answer/id #uuid "809f4ab8-4f1f-5e54-9203-8267e96a9d65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a^{\\frac{3}{2}},b-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517158} {:answer/id #uuid "ead9f7b1-0cb8-558f-874b-0ec2d56867ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2a,b-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ead9f7b1-0cb8-558f-874b-0ec2d56867ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2a,b-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517168} :answer-field/id #uuid "632c6568-7fc9-576e-9071-67a052ce8e78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517152}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "66562107-64e5-5b2b-99fc-50f565b62c27" :question/problem "The point $(a,b)$ lies on the curve $y=3^{x}.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=3^{x/2}-2?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=3^{x/2}-2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y=3^{x}.$\n\n- Then, we stretch the graph by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=3^{x/2}.$ After this transformation, the point $(a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2a,b).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $2$ units down and obtain the graph of $y=3^{x/2}-2.$ After this transformation, the point $(2a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2a,b-2).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(a,b)$ lies on the graph of $y=3^{x}$, we have that $3^{a}=b.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=3^{x/2}-2.$ Substituting $x=2a$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &=3^{\\frac{2a}{2}}-2 \\\\ &=3^{a}-2 \\\\ &=b-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(2a,b-2)$ must lie on $y=3^{x/2}-2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517159 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60e1147e-5dc9-5f98-96b0-207debfc66aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517151} {:answer/id #uuid "a79728f8-2e9b-58c9-8dc6-81f0885a0b3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517161} {:answer/id #uuid "c534cd05-2086-5e7c-922b-3cc6962ecdd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,12)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517163} {:answer/id #uuid "e9b36fe4-7e3c-5256-a2e6-98cdc8053553" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517166} {:answer/id #uuid "ea660efa-7758-5d3d-8e58-de56d6fe866e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,12)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9b36fe4-7e3c-5256-a2e6-98cdc8053553" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517166} :answer-field/id #uuid "487b85cb-7674-581b-8017-6af8479fe242" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517148}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9a93e41a-bed8-5a57-8ad1-e50ce41e47c6" :question/problem "The point $(-1,2)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)+7?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=-f(x)+7,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the graph across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,2)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1,-2).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $7$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+7.$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,-2)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1,5).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(-1,2)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$, we have that $f(-1)=2.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+7.$ Substituting $x=-1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &=-f(-1)+7 \\\\ &=-( 2 )+7 \\\\ &=-2+7 \\\\ &=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-1,5)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+7.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517169 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1932fa4d-0e84-5c29-bc33-ed320d2d5d11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517142} {:answer/id #uuid "27ef4273-5c13-5f92-82c9-a2b55222a870" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517144} {:answer/id #uuid "3fb2a799-b846-5d20-ba4a-f15e6b349abe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517147} {:answer/id #uuid "5683ad40-70e8-5a9e-bb6b-5299bd9ff4cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517150} {:answer/id #uuid "696d1a6c-6fc6-54d4-80b3-eb594b915464" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517155}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "696d1a6c-6fc6-54d4-80b3-eb594b915464" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517155} :answer-field/id #uuid "23c7f66d-fe13-5a2c-ab7c-e24bba1d00a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517143}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fe3500a7-c6d9-50ea-b641-628c05c03c77" :question/problem "The point $(-1,-2)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)-5?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=-f(x)-5,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the graph across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,-2)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1,2).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $5$ units down and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)-5.$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,2)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1,-3).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(-1,-2)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$, we have that $f(-1)=-2.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)-5.$ Substituting $x=-1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &=-f(-1)-5 \\\\ &=-( -2 )-5 \\\\ &=2-5 \\\\ &=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-1,-3)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)-5.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point That Lies on a Transformed Curve"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059669 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059435 :question/id #uuid "55fd531a-92e2-5f75-8c40-585d4f71bf4c" :question/math-academy-id "e-13412" :question/problem "The point $(3, 0)$ lies on the curve $y = f(x) - 1.$ What point **must** lie on $y = -f(x) + 3?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y = -f(x) + 3,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y = f(x) - 1.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph by $1$ unit up and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,0)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(3,0+1)=(3,1).\n$$\n\n- Next, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,1)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(3,-(1)) = (3,-1).\n$$\n\n- Finally, shift the result by $3$ units up and obtain the graph of $y = -f(x) + 3.$ After this transformation, the point $(3,-1)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(3,-1+3)=(3,2).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(3, 0)$ lies on the graph of $y = f(x)-1$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(3)-1 = 0\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nf(3)=1.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y = -f(x) + 3.$ Substituting $x = 3$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(3)+3 \\\\  & =-1+3 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(3, 2)$ must lie on $y = -f(x) + 3.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38b450bd-b43a-507c-9bbe-0aaf7a64a3ed" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060006 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "123d4004-5c38-5292-afe0-3926ee0d0bb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056395} {:answer/id #uuid "215d0348-3325-5b0f-b430-be17a1c52b8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2, 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056396} {:answer/id #uuid "37548807-d256-5fc9-98ef-bbc3e9fc2a85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2, 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056397} {:answer/id #uuid "5376b742-5988-5f01-98d6-a68f5ec75b57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056398} {:answer/id #uuid "5da47774-b4c3-5656-a5f3-c6639c1c26fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056399}] :answer-field/id #uuid "437656e2-e6a9-5e24-ad04-71e3bec79dec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056394}] :question/id #uuid "35f6ccdb-baf2-5629-ace1-fbbc52cdd1b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-121405" :question/problem "The point $(-2, 4)$ lies on the curve $y = 2f(x)$. Which of the following points **must** lie on $y =-f(x) + 2$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060259 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bad3011-eec4-5bd6-9f12-9de90977d847" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1, 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058177} {:answer/id #uuid "dba39df7-83e7-5fab-846e-119c803d8ee2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058178} {:answer/id #uuid "e1b7ee60-2fd8-523e-8b42-4824fc438dfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058179} {:answer/id #uuid "272b5946-719d-5b75-850b-893b6e80fde6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1, 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058180} {:answer/id #uuid "6edc1855-3018-5684-a7e0-1d2d3b236b62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058181}] :answer-field/id #uuid "0e9c2bb2-bb19-5c69-a07c-605a418546e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058176}] :question/id #uuid "49ac0e7b-6d64-5ad3-b75e-0edbe3724d3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-26595" :question/problem "The point $(-1, 1)$ lies on the curve $y = f(x) + 1$. Which of the following points **must** lie on $y =-f(x) - 1$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233290 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "66ee65e4-7115-5fe1-b74b-2344562d97c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-121431" :question/problem "The point  $(0,1)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x+2).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)+2?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(0,1)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=-f(x)+2:$\n\n- To plot the graph of $y=f(x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x+2).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally $2$ unit to the right  and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(0,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(0+2,1)=(2,1).\n$$\n\n- Next, to plot the graph of $y=-f(x)+2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,1)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(2,-(1))=(2,-1).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $2$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+2.$ After this transformation, the point $(2,-1)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(2,-1+2)=(2,1).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(0,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x+2)$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(0+2)=1\\,⟹\\,f(2)=1.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+2.$ Substituting $x=2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(2)+2 \\\\  & =-(1)+2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(2,1)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233535 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1b6d8898-251c-5832-a715-c1e0b2d67196" :question/math-academy-id "q-158542" :question/problem "The point  $(0,3)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x-1).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=f(x)+2?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(0,3)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=f(x)+2:$\n\n- To plot the graph of $y=f(x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x-1).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally $1$ unit to the left and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(0,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(0-1,3)=(-1,3).\n$$\n\n- Next, to plot the graph of $y=f(x)+2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $2$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=f(x)+2.$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,3)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(-1,3+2)=(-1,5).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(0,3)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x-1)$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(0-1)=3\\,⟹\\,f(-1)=3.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=f(x)+2.$ Substituting $x=-1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =f(-1)+2 \\\\  & =3+2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-1,5)$ must lie on $y=f(x)+2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233536 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bcbd4637-6a8f-52c9-a823-7c7a1c3283b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-158546" :question/problem "The point  $(2,1)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x-2).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=3-f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(2,1)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=3-f(x):$\n\n- To plot the graph of $y=f(x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x-2).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally $2$ units to the left and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2-2,1)=(0,1).\n$$\n\n- Next, to plot the graph of $y=3-f(x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(0,1)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(0,-(1))=(0,-1).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $3$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=3-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(0,-1)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(0,-1+3)=(0,2).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(2,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x-2)$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(2-2)=1\\,⟹\\,f(0)=1.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=3-f(x).$ Substituting $x=0$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3-f(0) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(0,2)$ must lie on $y=3-f(x).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233700 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d89896ee-0650-5ce4-90d0-9b6eb4318030" :question/math-academy-id "q-26597" :question/problem "The point  $(0,4)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x)+2.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)-3?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=-f(x)-3,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x)+2.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph vertically by $2$ unit down and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(0,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(0,4-2)=(0,2).\n$$\n\n- Next, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(0,2)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(0,-(2))=(0,-2).\n$$\n\n- Finally, shift the result vertically by $3$ units down and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)-3.$ After this transformation, the point $(0,-2)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(0,-2-3)=(0,-5).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(0,4)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)+2$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(0)+2=4\\,⟹\\,f(0)=2.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)-3.$ Substituting $x=0$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(0)-3 \\\\  & =-2-3 \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(0,-5)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)-3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253566 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42a70a80-9905-5343-a34c-136a375e25ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251108} {:answer/id #uuid "9d7c8745-888b-5fc6-9d1a-fc1686129698" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251913} {:answer/id #uuid "a8ce837a-8964-565e-9e2d-f825bac56dba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252014} {:answer/id #uuid "d6b4c14b-cbd8-5494-b7e3-3692ec97eb35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252455} {:answer/id #uuid "e0fa8370-7c51-5755-8595-d094b48e3a78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252534}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d7c8745-888b-5fc6-9d1a-fc1686129698" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251913} :answer-field/id #uuid "2019c876-575f-5be0-a410-079acd66193f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252863}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4d38949b-9d50-5e0f-bb63-ea8ff36967c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-119308" :question/problem "The point $(2,3)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x)-1.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)+4?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=-f(x)+4,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x)-1.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph vertically by $1$ unit up and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2,3+1)=(2,4).\n$$\n\n- Next, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,4)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(2,-(4))=(2,-4).\n$$\n\n- Finally, shift the result vertically by $4$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+4.$ After this transformation, the point $(2,-4)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(2,-4+4)=(2,0).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(2,3)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)-1$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(2)-1=3\\,⟹\\,f(2)=4.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+4.$ Substituting $x=2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(2)+4 \\\\  & =-4+4 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(2,0)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256542 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34d52287-c3ec-5c1d-bc8c-f42f5d1b1173" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254122} {:answer/id #uuid "48193e72-9f23-5387-b843-0b954ae77279" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254280} {:answer/id #uuid "775e9c8f-aa49-5c77-bf40-dadfb16663be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254726} {:answer/id #uuid "83e30582-b220-5945-90a6-dc420162278d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254842} {:answer/id #uuid "df43e300-d37a-5ddf-bd7d-9b8787a369ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255652}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48193e72-9f23-5387-b843-0b954ae77279" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254280} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0cc13c9-29f6-55db-9718-7dfe7fa0c982" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256348}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "558ef869-beed-50a3-ac33-63c76838d181" :question/math-academy-id "q-121407" :question/problem "The point  $(-3,4)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x+1).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)+2?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(-3,4)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=-f(x)+2:$\n\n- To plot the graph of $y=f(x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x+1).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally $1$ unit to the right  and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-3,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-3+1,4)=(-2,4).\n$$\n\n- Next, to plot the graph of $y=-f(x)+2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,4)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(-2,-(4))=(-2,-4).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $2$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+2.$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,-4)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(-2,-4+2)=(-2,-2).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(-3,4)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x+1)$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(-3+1)=4\\,⟹\\,f(-2)=4.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+2.$ Substituting $x=-2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(-2)+2 \\\\  & =-4+2 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-2,-2)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256543 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30f927d8-7469-5f46-bcd8-ceb6220bf583" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254097} {:answer/id #uuid "5679b0cb-b917-5834-9cfc-21236c05bf34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254431} {:answer/id #uuid "8ddf4522-8bdc-5052-93ac-81ce5d766651" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254921} {:answer/id #uuid "d96d1372-c430-5db2-a838-5534030331cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255597} {:answer/id #uuid "dc8c348e-dc48-52a8-a7a2-dcc717c081b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255624}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30f927d8-7469-5f46-bcd8-ceb6220bf583" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254097} :answer-field/id #uuid "65ce4959-7406-5c8f-b9dc-e08ef35e4173" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256112}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e35c2dec-10c0-57fb-accb-533118e28579" :question/math-academy-id "q-121414" :question/problem "The point  $(4,8)$ lies on the curve $y=4f(x).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)+3?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(4,8)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=-f(x)+3:$\n\n- To plot the graph of $y=f(x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=4f(x).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the graph by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{4}$ parallel to the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(4,8)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(4,\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 8)=(4,2).\n$$\n\n- Next, to plot the graph of $y=-f(x)+3,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(4,2)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(4,-(2))=(4,-2).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $3$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+3.$ After this transformation, the point $(4,-2)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(4,-2+3)=(4,1).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(4,8)$ lies on the graph of $y=4f(x)$, we have that \n\n$$\n4f(4)=8\\,⟹\\,f(4)=2.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+3.$ Substituting $x=4$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(4)+3 \\\\  & =-2+3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(4,1)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256544 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b36618b-e713-53a3-ac40-6a8f5599cd4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254899} {:answer/id #uuid "a4b1c168-5fba-5920-9504-8801e7ea3407" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255124} {:answer/id #uuid "b929824d-a081-5202-b41a-f097c3bbd269" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255313} {:answer/id #uuid "b939c68f-9f1f-5b41-b64e-a890960575a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255316} {:answer/id #uuid "ddeface4-f07f-5f2a-bf40-5f85d82a4184" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255637}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4b1c168-5fba-5920-9504-8801e7ea3407" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255124} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3896048-4abe-53e6-9858-4e169d6c5730" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256246}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "517a1517-e6eb-55b1-8c36-85bba8616819" :question/math-academy-id "q-121418" :question/problem "The point  $(2,3)$ lies on the curve $y=\\frac{1}{3}f(x).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)+5?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(2,3)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=-f(x)+5:$\n\n- To plot the graph of $y=f(x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=\\frac{1}{3}f(x).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the graph by a scale factor of $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2,3\\cdot 3)=(2,9).\n$$\n\n- Next, to plot the graph of $y=-f(x)+5,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,9)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(2,-(9))=(2,-9).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $5$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+5.$ After this transformation, the point $(2,-9)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(2,-9+5)=(2,-4).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(2,3)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{3}f(x)$, we have that \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}f(2)=3\\,⟹\\,f(2)=9.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+5.$ Substituting $x=2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(2)+5 \\\\  & =-(9)+5 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(2,-4)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+5.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292650 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "036b3467-551b-5d3e-99f8-f34df741c278" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290655} {:answer/id #uuid "1789665b-9485-59bf-ac4a-24f4ca8e7c6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290731} {:answer/id #uuid "4d22bf21-6fd1-521b-8cf6-ef9303eb6596" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290960} {:answer/id #uuid "8d7a2478-3275-509d-a6c9-6e581e21989f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291237} {:answer/id #uuid "d66724d1-9c6b-5173-a28b-c720d8d3a432" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291530}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "036b3467-551b-5d3e-99f8-f34df741c278" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290655} :answer-field/id #uuid "20dcd5d3-0f90-560e-bed2-53e688d6b92b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291856}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "145a9fb2-d278-526f-9014-c06bdf23b5a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-26408" :question/problem "The point $(-1,3)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x)+1.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)+2?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=-f(x)+2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x)+1.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph vertically by $1$ unit down and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1,3-1)=(-1,2).\n$$\n\n- Next, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,2)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(-1,-(2))=(-1,-2).\n$$\n\n- Finally, shift the result vertically by $2$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+2.$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,-2)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(-1,-2+2)=(-1,0).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(-1,3)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)+1$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(-1)+1=3\\,⟹\\,f(-1)=2.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+2.$ Substituting $x=-1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(-1)+2 \\\\  & =-2+2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-1,0)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292673 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f5bb3ff-2bf2-59a6-a9dc-028e36b29e5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290821} {:answer/id #uuid "6a0dffee-d291-5706-b099-bf26056b0a79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291085} {:answer/id #uuid "745f11b3-4abb-5778-b95c-05bfed92c9ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291130} {:answer/id #uuid "add1ba6b-56e1-507d-81c4-d7cc7dd66d71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291387} {:answer/id #uuid "f811fc7c-86fc-528d-a6a8-cba9ceee139f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "add1ba6b-56e1-507d-81c4-d7cc7dd66d71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291387} :answer-field/id #uuid "e46bb93d-f096-5e20-be4c-e4f183d28feb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292449}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4ee92c98-a45f-55db-8092-7db7c00e5a4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-26596" :question/problem "The point  $(3,2)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x)-2.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)+2?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(3,2)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=-f(x)+2:$\n\n- To plot the graph of $y=f(x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x)-2.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph vertically by $2$ units up and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,2)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(3,2+2)=(3,4).\n$$\n\n- Next, to plot the graph of $y=-f(x)+2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,4)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(3,-(4))=(3,-4).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $2$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+2.$ After this transformation, the point $(3,-4)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(3,-4+2)=(3,-2).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(3,2)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)-2$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(3)-2=2\\,⟹\\,f(3)=4.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+2.$ Substituting $x=3$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(3)+2 \\\\  & =-4+2 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(3,-2)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292679 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e45f316-de9f-5078-a264-4bec8ddfa33d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291105} {:answer/id #uuid "81083a07-3ca9-51a3-aef1-cacbb9dc674e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291178} {:answer/id #uuid "818351c2-9cf1-5db6-910a-2037182a0665" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291179} {:answer/id #uuid "d8f07a2a-5cfb-5180-a1fd-c1ba356692a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291540} {:answer/id #uuid "f8be8001-d31c-55ee-acbf-2ca45e8e2140" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291681}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81083a07-3ca9-51a3-aef1-cacbb9dc674e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291178} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4fe658c-44a8-558f-a2d7-7429144d854f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292500}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d646706a-b186-5587-8de9-3f31bf5fbf11" :question/math-academy-id "q-26726" :question/problem "The point  $(4,2)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x)+2.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)+4?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=-f(x)+4,$ we apply the following steps:  \n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x)+2.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph vertically by $2$ units down and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(4,2)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(4,2-2)=(4,0).\n$$\n\n- Next, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(4,0)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(4,-(0))=(4,0).\n$$\n\n- Finally, shift the result vertically by $4$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+4.$ After this transformation, the point $(4,0)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(4,0+4)=(4,4).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(4,2)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)+2$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(4)+2=2\\,⟹\\,f(4)=0.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+4.$ Substituting $x=4$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(4)+4 \\\\  & =-0+4 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(4,4)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517133 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a9495a1-4221-5b47-9412-f22032097e13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517123} {:answer/id #uuid "6237cdec-071a-51a2-acb5-f9adfb6e874c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517125} {:answer/id #uuid "d8770e35-e2a3-50d5-be73-804751858bee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517136} {:answer/id #uuid "e3d7550c-e25d-5ef7-964e-f5fa32a57502" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517137} {:answer/id #uuid "f7dbd166-b5b2-5ec2-a66a-c579223ce6ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\frac{1}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517139}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6237cdec-071a-51a2-acb5-f9adfb6e874c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517125} :answer-field/id #uuid "9316bd9c-bd92-574c-a31a-91f35278e63e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517127}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b55e01e5-9c24-510f-825f-e89bf23a1ddf" :question/problem "The point $(1,2)$ lies on the curve $y=\\frac{1}{3}f(x).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)-4?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(1,2)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=-f(x)-4:$\n\n- First, take the graph $y=\\frac{1}{3}f(x).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the graph by a scale factor of $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(1,2)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1,3\\cdot (2))=(1,6).\n$$\n\n- Next, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(1,6)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1,-(6))=(1,-6).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $4$ units down and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)-4.$ After this transformation, the point $(1,-6)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1,-6-4)=(1,-10).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(1,2)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{3}f(x),$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}f(1)=2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad f(1)=6.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)-4.$ Substituting $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y&=-f(1)-4\\\\&=-(6)-4\\\\&=-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(1,-10)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)-4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517134 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d286aa7-b2d6-58d2-bf29-61fb1720d50e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-7,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517121} {:answer/id #uuid "48f83f88-0613-5776-915c-a5994a6fc5f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(7,-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517122} {:answer/id #uuid "61e8efb1-edb5-5857-a2e3-2d735f94c05b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(7,-16)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517124} {:answer/id #uuid "65e4c577-9efa-5639-9d6b-751511f27cdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-7,-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517126} {:answer/id #uuid "d79a5116-a0ab-50fa-a0de-8d76eaba32ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-7,16)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517135}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65e4c577-9efa-5639-9d6b-751511f27cdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-7,-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517126} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2bc82bb-e2ed-5d33-b951-229f9e8624e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517132}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b988807a-34eb-52af-995a-9e3c8a1f0e2f" :question/problem "The point $(-7,8)$ lies on the curve $y=2f(x).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)-4?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(-7,8)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=-f(x)-4:$\n\n- First, take the graph $y=2f(x).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the graph by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-7,8)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-7,\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (8))=(-7,4).\n$$\n\n- Next, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-7,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-7,-(4))=(-7,-4).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $4$ units down and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)-4.$ After this transformation, the point $(-7,-4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-7,-4-4)=(-7,-8).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(-7,8)$ lies on the graph of $y=2f(x),$ we have that\n\n$$\n2f(-7)=8\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad f(-7)=4.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)-4.$ Substituting $x=-7,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y&=-f(-7)-4\\\\&=-(4)-4\\\\&=-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-7,-8)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)-4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517140 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11b3afc9-f9cc-5c8a-b6ab-4390c55f86f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(8,- \\frac{27}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517120} {:answer/id #uuid "97d37dda-73f6-5def-b61e-7b30f2c18eb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-8,- \\frac{27}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517129} {:answer/id #uuid "98fc6441-004a-50b8-be13-2c8d2fa211bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-8,- \\frac{27}{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517130} {:answer/id #uuid "b05f20f9-ffc8-5085-b0e1-8fa22e17eee0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(8,- \\frac{27}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517131} {:answer/id #uuid "e6f14205-061b-58e6-8ec2-15a66df7a546" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-8,\\frac{27}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517138}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97d37dda-73f6-5def-b61e-7b30f2c18eb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-8,- \\frac{27}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517129} :answer-field/id #uuid "95633b9d-4569-53c0-963e-77e08465b5f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517128}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f8cb0f82-4ced-5ad3-8499-c17a38448a7a" :question/problem "The point $(-8,7)$ lies on the curve $y=4f(x).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)-5?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(-8,7)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=-f(x)-5:$\n\n- First, take the graph $y=4f(x).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the graph by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{4}$ parallel to the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-8,7)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-8,\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (7))=(-8,\\frac{7}{4}).\n$$\n\n- Next, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-8,\\frac{7}{4})$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-8,-(\\frac{7}{4}))=(-8,- \\frac{7}{4}).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $5$ units down and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)-5.$ After this transformation, the point $(-8,- \\frac{7}{4})$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-8,- \\frac{7}{4}-5)=(-8,- \\frac{27}{4}).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(-8,7)$ lies on the graph of $y=4f(x),$ we have that\n\n$$\n4f(-8)=7\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad f(-8)=\\frac{7}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)-5.$ Substituting $x=-8,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y&=-f(-8)-5\\\\&=-(\\frac{7}{4})-5\\\\&=- \\frac{27}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-8,- \\frac{27}{4})$ must lie on $y=-f(x)-5.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point That Lies on a Transformed Curve Given Another Transformed Curve"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110755 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110457 :question/id #uuid "02c747c2-0eda-5c53-8e43-829f878dd278" :question/math-academy-id "e-3482" :question/problem "![](images/aa/aad0b38f5afb88c13039ac577a927e232d84905de40297ed824c966795ce7bb4.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\dfrac{2}{3},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin\\theta.$" :question/worked-solution "Any point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\dfrac{2}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{2}{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{4}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{5}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $\\sin\\theta$ is positive in the first quadrant. \n\nSo our final answer is $\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 5}{3}.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c73db44-2ec2-5025-9431-acb02e414916" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050291 :topic/math-academy-id 112 :topic/title "Properties of the Unit Circle in the First Quadrant"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111158 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b2b084c-2030-569c-b3db-c1f0bb240497" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108674} {:answer/id #uuid "a1178c63-4702-5bdc-9e41-c3046acb6f63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108675} {:answer/id #uuid "d3dca092-57eb-5e07-8d0d-0d53376fcddf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108676} {:answer/id #uuid "ec1a8afd-47ad-5ee5-aef2-253b8da8405f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108677} {:answer/id #uuid "f8b6b5a3-b768-5bb1-8d7e-9d7a7b3b6af1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108678}] :answer-field/id #uuid "af420e31-aeb4-5e79-ae37-c4b53833e453" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108673}] :question/id #uuid "6d39d8f8-c6eb-55ec-8766-ad9675623f6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-37821" :question/problem "![](images/d5/d51a8e78f8b8c1f05e502bd98aed6a253799336765f1331d23e63ec637bcb448.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}$, use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin θ$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111338 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "858b163e-06a9-58cd-9a79-e8996dde982a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109754} {:answer/id #uuid "d4616041-b74c-517b-82b9-81605e9dba4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109755} {:answer/id #uuid "040fdf42-5db8-5bf1-97e5-7716e2cd61a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109756} {:answer/id #uuid "1cd2797a-205a-5efb-bcd2-69aacd7ecc5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109757} {:answer/id #uuid "4c2c194a-9614-50f8-8038-fbd740ae0151" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109758}] :answer-field/id #uuid "6398bb17-d95c-582c-89e7-0e3607f149b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109753}] :question/id #uuid "f003c458-ef4c-5282-91a8-b25d1a9a217c" :question/math-academy-id "q-64731" :question/problem "![](images/cb/cbff0c974c790ec4287be1dcff8aa62d0da0aa5e77fa5ec577633f5f2cad6a5d.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$, use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\cos θ$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259697 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1899aeff-6c99-5c03-9546-b76ef39af986" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256995} {:answer/id #uuid "3d869581-ecbb-520d-8a4e-930bb77c4ffd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257329} {:answer/id #uuid "6473b1ab-aae1-5e0b-a569-58d6da31fd9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257665} {:answer/id #uuid "8fc46843-1ec1-59a1-bcce-8c1afc2c6e65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258046} {:answer/id #uuid "a6cbcfd0-4ae8-5672-951c-dc2e216fa8f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258238}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6cbcfd0-4ae8-5672-951c-dc2e216fa8f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258238} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2385214-bc0d-583b-b908-7acf965a5700" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259317}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "371bdeca-3a35-5956-9fa6-e8d03505d26b" :question/math-academy-id "q-124625" :question/problem "![](images/e5/e50a755a20330d7f0eca8d02c8ddab33f392d5b70f7ca35b4dc514e22e61141c.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{6}{7},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/fb/fb0da765a857607ce0c8f13690efaa2f0d504ccaa51899a23044ee4ed17f0aa5.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{6}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡\\theta =\\frac{6}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{6}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{6}{7})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{36}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{13}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So our final answer is $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323955 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a4cafde-db4c-54bb-bab3-013f762c6d9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321731} {:answer/id #uuid "4e99ea8e-9a16-57a0-baea-c6ae781ca29a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321765} {:answer/id #uuid "5cf875a9-930b-5577-b821-7ead99c40d06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321884} {:answer/id #uuid "7fe73b65-ebc3-5cff-9a52-a69da7741fb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322150} {:answer/id #uuid "b124652f-7ab9-5b4c-9413-d3dfdcb4c6a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322535}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e99ea8e-9a16-57a0-baea-c6ae781ca29a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321765} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce29bc07-9e48-58fd-9669-3eeb517f79e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323572}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "565688d3-a42b-5b17-9265-a372de4b8754" :question/math-academy-id "q-37899" :question/problem "![](images/f4/f4fdf6f8d819a4ee043b1cc3b86d795e3166ebfe181e0958246dc084c2f9c6fc.png)\n\nThe point $P$ lies on the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of $P$ is $\\frac{3}{7},$ use the Pythagorean Identity to find $sin⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/cb/cb569d4b7b76699259d2167b8c930e636e3fb794b14ef25a4d909c224235d7c3.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{3}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{3}{7})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{9}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{40}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So our final answer is $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323957 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d8126b5-2054-5d88-865f-be6cbe674d45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321368} {:answer/id #uuid "3466518b-1130-594a-9f91-8439ec16fa46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321544} {:answer/id #uuid "51eee8c3-23ea-5613-a452-4c2182222cb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321794} {:answer/id #uuid "5ba4b8d7-052a-5cf2-86db-50636fe22d83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321871} {:answer/id #uuid "c2131ada-4ea2-5f29-85f1-aa42dc442a53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322653}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3466518b-1130-594a-9f91-8439ec16fa46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321544} :answer-field/id #uuid "617b107f-dc16-5899-aab5-ab0f5850cc8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323364}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9112180a-ac6b-5472-ba8b-819d346ead1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-37915" :question/problem "![](images/6c/6c36dbce847c719accc77fa15f33dfb9a87cfae669691afe30f7693663076d2e.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/52/52fe94bb303a001e89f9db221e9730d4ca8c5c284217aa20fb6084ee041dec66.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{(\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =2-2(\\frac{10}{16}) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{20}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our final answer is $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335735 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d900898-f644-59cb-8bd9-292f8cf96161" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333108} {:answer/id #uuid "128843bf-c0d0-5b0b-9af7-5c0891c02535" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333155} {:answer/id #uuid "867d9b75-2324-5dfe-830f-942376886bea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334183} {:answer/id #uuid "de2d463a-06e0-5a98-b913-8a3a09131403" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334926} {:answer/id #uuid "f58082e0-41ed-5179-bfb1-b391fbd71bba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335143}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "867d9b75-2324-5dfe-830f-942376886bea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334183} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4eb5247-b68b-5a82-8180-4c0ffe3de0aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335575}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e9c7086f-08af-5d07-8de2-8cc3da9eb077" :question/math-academy-id "q-64681" :question/problem "![](images/94/94b8650466747f6858b30adbb8962e6c91159466b570f536d4999e2b08c31b67.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{1}{2},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/cb/cb569d4b7b76699259d2167b8c930e636e3fb794b14ef25a4d909c224235d7c3.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So our final answer is $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335783 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ebb8345-8448-5af3-a7b5-f875fc57e26d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{55}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333119} {:answer/id #uuid "87f8bdf1-b693-5449-95dc-41601ab98dee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334192} {:answer/id #uuid "9cd4a426-2676-5c7e-b66d-c6dd8478bed9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{56}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334369}