nFinally, we select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So our final answer is $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{14}}{9}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511686 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "343c6fe7-74c0-52a2-bc86-d208dbf32b4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511689} {:answer/id #uuid "5b5a672c-e194-5807-a342-905cf49227f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511693} {:answer/id #uuid "75399b50-a49f-5812-89c2-ed1173a8611d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511696} {:answer/id #uuid "c39a656b-c2c8-5877-9d30-c0d08addf19d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511706} {:answer/id #uuid "ead3e7bc-0d8f-5814-a629-e38097f034cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511710}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "343c6fe7-74c0-52a2-bc86-d208dbf32b4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511689} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a5d9b12-19cd-584b-b779-a468e4d6ca2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511691}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2c5094d1-3c19-540e-a83e-e53a0c0e885b" :question/problem "![](images/9a/9a6e00e08f331baf932661a341627dd6403ca5c33f17b5fe51f2343198c1b5f1.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{7}{8},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/60/608cde6d839f956b950438c50f54338fb7ec7b816ffc21ac38515dbf79a9f980.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{7}{8}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡\\theta =\\frac{7}{8}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{7}{8}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{7}{8})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{49}{64}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{15}{64}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So our final answer is $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{8}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511687 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a32365c-3ae5-53ac-a149-f47a33daf7c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511683} {:answer/id #uuid "931680d4-ddc2-5fe3-a179-0b349fd50d72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{38}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511700} {:answer/id #uuid "ab910f87-b9dc-573a-978e-cb0375562e3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{76}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511703} {:answer/id #uuid "e5f2325b-f13b-5140-9d7e-7661e658a40f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{38}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511709} {:answer/id #uuid "f4058ccc-66d9-56ac-a956-faf95687a1e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511712}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "931680d4-ddc2-5fe3-a179-0b349fd50d72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{38}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511700} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b7f5f26-505c-5de5-95d8-c1e0ccb5a7c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511694}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "31b52705-15c7-508e-bc5b-87541ef55e38" :question/problem "![](images/22/2242f7a5c4b3386e8f493799e71645145e9290fa5eaad66f986931d9e38006e2.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{5},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/a6/a6a3acf1948838a3d1d8f0effe0e1cdb30282b289f89009cbbcac716b4b73700.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =2-2(\\frac{6}{25}) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{12}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{38}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our final answer is $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{38}{25}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511701 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b266fd5-0108-5d1b-b55c-e7ab7da30986" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511682} {:answer/id #uuid "3231f0e8-0e93-53ea-825c-432c55cfd011" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{128}{49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511688} {:answer/id #uuid "38075e8b-f56a-5110-8581-0f19e65d1cb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511690} {:answer/id #uuid "6c632c64-803b-5920-ba44-0bd15d2842da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511695} {:answer/id #uuid "a47751bb-fbec-549f-b413-287c65e22ec5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{49}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511702}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38075e8b-f56a-5110-8581-0f19e65d1cb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511690} :answer-field/id #uuid "85df7c4c-736c-5b18-b67c-bc225c6084a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511697}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9e339c15-6319-50b0-b160-b0fbf1d94d58" :question/problem "![](images/e0/e0cdc84ef38e2c2074bdab95458a97452209858b74a370b65bd7f393c1bde908.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{7},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/69/69ab1b2e23a2815343db2b914204aa5864e73254dd1aa22d501c08b8ae155714.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{(\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{7})}^{2} \\\\  & =2-2(\\frac{17}{49}) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{34}{49} \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{49}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our final answer is $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{64}{49}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511705 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "037cbbc0-15eb-5ca5-9687-929e9c03c3b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511681} {:answer/id #uuid "5524e754-213c-5297-b6c8-38ca120bb015" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511692} {:answer/id #uuid "8dc4b2b4-ef85-5136-ae4f-475a2d6e7ccc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511698} {:answer/id #uuid "8e1ea995-d6ac-507a-b61c-f3087c637df3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511699} {:answer/id #uuid "fd2e1cc1-cb15-5e19-b2ed-06cfc90ef286" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd2e1cc1-cb15-5e19-b2ed-06cfc90ef286" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511714} :answer-field/id #uuid "1aa6be0a-2bd9-5771-ae42-ec9c7d256501" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511684}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ba9e8819-628f-5c44-89ce-c66c0188feda" :question/problem "![](images/bd/bd7cf164aa9ac00697292aabf03d616b62b216014ac00a0f36e9194ad7685ce2.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/df/df6444faa947e137efc95beeb24c59e8e685830096bf4019fef776abd87c4bff.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6})}^{2} \\\\  & =2-2(\\frac{6}{36}) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{12}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our final answer is $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511708 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02858282-63f7-5705-8f8e-00f513fcf0a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{48}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511680} {:answer/id #uuid "b84e32c1-da66-5b76-9568-280c26d49655" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511704} {:answer/id #uuid "d39f7a6b-0199-5e05-a5c4-d51a6cc4d9f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511707} {:answer/id #uuid "f13958bf-0d8f-555a-bf51-2bf79723a49b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511711} {:answer/id #uuid "fbd43435-b072-5f01-b302-1d59e6fd5742" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511713}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbd43435-b072-5f01-b302-1d59e6fd5742" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511713} :answer-field/id #uuid "21996560-b703-53d3-bfc5-aac31475eee5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511685}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd4c56b2-c037-5749-b1cc-f43f2eb74d1e" :question/problem "![](images/a3/a35a31ccce6ce9e50c386b920c8c4b0fecf23a4f29741e982dcd6c69bf38453c.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{5},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/09/0961629419accd50af36ce88aecafe33ea264d278d18d6f71c4aa4d76c06be97.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{(\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =2-2(\\frac{13}{25}) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{26}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our final answer is $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{24}{25}.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110758 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110459 :question/id #uuid "ba6cc6a0-dbb8-58d2-9d02-c76310028d4e" :question/math-academy-id "e-3485" :question/problem "![](images/e6/e666ddc8a8eda452abd833e963446c96de5d34dfbf76e9c92564e85a48080524.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\dfrac{4}{5},$ what is $\\tan\\theta?$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=\\dfrac{4}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{4}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\sin\\theta$ is positive in the first quadrant. Therefore $\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{3}{5}.$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta},\n$$\n\nwe finally get\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right)} = \\dfrac {3}{4}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74d05ad6-7e4a-5c50-92d1-ca4b2f00cbb1" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111339 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d58f0d3a-48cb-5dc6-87a4-afe1cfebcbfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109760} {:answer/id #uuid "db8dde9f-3c21-548d-8ee7-573fad3133d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109761} {:answer/id #uuid "de137338-a214-5e28-b644-3e94e4f16484" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109762} {:answer/id #uuid "08b8a4ea-1821-5bba-8395-25d188efb2cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109763} {:answer/id #uuid "152363af-0269-58b7-b058-87a6b12cc37c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109764}] :answer-field/id #uuid "a416b38a-3b4c-5694-8ff4-47b0c91eb2c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109759}] :question/id #uuid "8a1f92d7-1b72-57f9-b8bc-455d27a59aca" :question/math-academy-id "q-64974" :question/problem "![](images/1b/1b084e0ad229e935d70d751f0e0ddaacf5832017951a7ef8e334f27834662d5c.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{2}{3}$, use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\cot θ$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111342 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f5011e1-5a24-51a6-a560-84948c0866a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333123} {:answer/id #uuid "1a630eb5-70c5-5ded-bed1-6c97af35f229" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{45}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333231} {:answer/id #uuid "72e4ea13-feef-5acf-9b9a-0e579d2765d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334027} {:answer/id #uuid "c360afef-02b3-5232-b951-ae761d89057d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334684} {:answer/id #uuid "fc79cebb-683f-5ec6-b279-a40dd479a779" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335208}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72e4ea13-feef-5acf-9b9a-0e579d2765d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334027} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a1eb48a-b984-5dff-bbdc-6a2962f92e64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335352}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0efeeff3-47fe-5165-905e-7ef041b1d387" :question/math-academy-id "q-65073" :question/problem "![](images/96/96b22799c75a4b19df4c6bddf8b63d11a7d0cab8a0cc481b8466a29eb1a2af15.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $tan⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/21/21170c11aa5ff0ab39b86f94588e5e8a0fa178d0c34b9aa1774ee9f07a4d0812.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{45}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{4}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{7}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7})}{(\\frac{2}{7})}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259732 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0be5fee5-ff9c-5713-906a-6d90f5a9cb0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{54}}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256895} {:answer/id #uuid "15efda3b-3678-57d9-8d54-1db2e510c65d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256978} {:answer/id #uuid "26ace001-a008-507d-a0e9-16b26952290c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{154}}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257114} {:answer/id #uuid "2c0cbc61-b93d-54e4-b926-1de5e2999404" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257176} {:answer/id #uuid "60c8fc36-e289-5c4e-a0d0-f7e4ddae3e65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{154}}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257633}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60c8fc36-e289-5c4e-a0d0-f7e4ddae3e65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{154}}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257633} :answer-field/id #uuid "80a8e351-07c7-5816-954b-d7240ccbef42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259253}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "adb42fa3-fa6c-5e9a-800e-ffdd8192d291" :question/math-academy-id "q-124789" :question/problem "![](images/aa/aad0b38f5afb88c13039ac577a927e232d84905de40297ed824c966795ce7bb4.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{5},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $tan⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/e2/e2323a87ec4ab790714f4f6f5e5ff359495e93c6be5d76ae4b9f7ff15d14fd90.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{14}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{5}.$\n\nKnowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{5})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{5})}=\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{\\sqrt{11}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{\\sqrt{11}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{\\sqrt{11}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{\\sqrt{11}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{14\\cdot 11}}{11} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{154}}{11}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259733 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "148d9fa6-e709-5ed3-8dee-a9794d49d279" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256967} {:answer/id #uuid "57004998-41ca-5e4e-90f5-2c24561a08c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257531} {:answer/id #uuid "7ee702e6-7a9d-5c39-9f0f-93b8b0fe4698" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{35}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257913} {:answer/id #uuid "d244da59-6698-5edd-a3a0-8e822fc76895" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258615} {:answer/id #uuid "d255b826-a073-5d0c-8306-dc2845cf337e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258617}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d255b826-a073-5d0c-8306-dc2845cf337e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258617} :answer-field/id #uuid "8654efa3-cd29-5e20-bbaf-04bd03e93e30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259265}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dba2d6f5-ab4c-55d2-aafd-8aaa2c0dbeec" :question/math-academy-id "q-124791" :question/problem "![](images/c2/c2938adc4474cec0da06b5cee00b60110b7e7be86d2b0cec3f1cff99653b8f76.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5},$ what is $tan⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/af/af76b605cad900f45bc9def13e9ec2ffb12cb253ff0bdd4c07e764eac2a30937.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{12}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{13}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. Therefore $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{5}.$\n\nKnowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{5})}{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5})}=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{13\\cdot 3}}{2\\cdot 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259734 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "030abc43-15ab-542a-9efc-a5b8959e8901" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256806} {:answer/id #uuid "164f1f56-0609-51b8-a92f-5f6f8b2829eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256984} {:answer/id #uuid "b606a516-6e71-50b3-b37f-00403c1f1485" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258363} {:answer/id #uuid "bae703ab-5b1b-5842-8663-291050f98b09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258418} {:answer/id #uuid "f3d72a9c-545b-5d53-89de-2aadd8702aa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258913}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3d72a9c-545b-5d53-89de-2aadd8702aa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258913} :answer-field/id #uuid "e79ad56d-ed57-5258-b07c-74c9f1f477d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259438}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9926f7c6-9c2e-5943-b7e8-596de1b982b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-124796" :question/problem "![](images/ee/ee92f92a52d8ade2052a62a5c19906cd86027b47f22ec986a740b9379e66d8d1.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{1}{8},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "Any point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{1}{8}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{8}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{1}{8})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{64}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{63}{64}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{8}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{8})}{(\\frac{1}{8})}=3\\sqrt{7}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335738 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07056c3e-506d-5641-9888-b4b318e25eff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333058} {:answer/id #uuid "a034f5c0-3b14-5e29-8def-edc95efbd80e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334397} {:answer/id #uuid "cb74b243-d73b-5089-bae4-f981f0a60cd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334760} {:answer/id #uuid "d2ffa90f-9b1f-53a0-9ae4-72f9fe2ebb09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334836} {:answer/id #uuid "d8504233-ad25-5121-8f69-909d3fab6b8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334884}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07056c3e-506d-5641-9888-b4b318e25eff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333058} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b238c5f-814a-5a46-a972-3a25a21b520c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335319}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8585ed26-d09c-5c8e-84bf-c2d2c03840ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-64730" :question/problem "![](images/2f/2f01b946a5c7c0f93c2171379603c4426913af5a15d0bc03434785144cfe4102.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},$ what is $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "Any point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{2}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. Therefore $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\nUsing the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we finally get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}=1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335789 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "194eaee8-54cb-563d-8f6d-d34f552da442" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333221} {:answer/id #uuid "8dcc27a4-b6ff-5196-bfc9-478bbd73a589" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334253} {:answer/id #uuid "a96bc338-b51a-5af0-adad-0e96975b2300" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334473} {:answer/id #uuid "ad3570c1-3d28-5add-bb53-9c8895491a2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334516} {:answer/id #uuid "e6417934-5eda-53e4-82d9-b2e434d37de6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334996}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad3570c1-3d28-5add-bb53-9c8895491a2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334516} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfce3d7a-f48e-534b-a3f3-76cd7412d6ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335631}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7581029a-054b-5d43-92f5-a2b561001b1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-65072" :question/problem "![](images/e6/e666ddc8a8eda452abd833e963446c96de5d34dfbf76e9c92564e85a48080524.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{3}{5},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $tan⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/fc/fc291f1fc5c4a712d3eef7ea2872de621855669d97445b24a2bc7a9da55949d9.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{3}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{4}{5}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{5})}{(\\frac{4}{5})}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335790 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b84d4ec-9f5f-5a47-8517-3bb8f8c9e6f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333094} {:answer/id #uuid "0f97daea-fc44-5b9a-b6c8-8ae1ec14226d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\sqrt{17}}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333127} {:answer/id #uuid "26887548-0fc3-51f2-9193-efc1f3262179" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333343} {:answer/id #uuid "9f0d2d95-0c48-5abf-8954-df6ad2d3263b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334390} {:answer/id #uuid "c3781df0-cce1-500d-ae05-5955c50dfe45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334685}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3781df0-cce1-500d-ae05-5955c50dfe45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334685} :answer-field/id #uuid "360fb473-e8de-57ee-bab2-0cdf46873b28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335346}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5aa7dee2-18db-5dfd-b251-d711e6634ce2" :question/math-academy-id "q-65075" :question/problem "![](images/d8/d846227996dd5fc1d6c3ca30108f548ac0d3743ec9262db0a551d28a32386996.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{9},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $cot⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/52/5284ae5a30a5d370d52c887cc6222ee4991a98d087dc751cbcfe5d7307ca1d47.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{9}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{9}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{9}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{9})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{17}{81}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{64}{81}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{8}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{8}{9}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $cot⁡\\theta =\\frac{cos⁡\\theta }{sin⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ncot⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{9})}{(\\frac{8}{9})}=\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{8}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510727 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "249f6d39-c352-5b6e-b0a3-aad8dbe4af0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510728} {:answer/id #uuid "40e4413d-62c5-5cf3-8a6d-bc0951e1d6d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{83}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510731} {:answer/id #uuid "9ae139e3-a55e-5972-9da7-3d735e750073" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510738} {:answer/id #uuid "cf16047a-f770-52e9-8ba0-da97bd2d6088" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{258}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510745} {:answer/id #uuid "eaa164a1-1ef9-5a0d-9231-f146dcc770a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{258}}{43}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510747}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf16047a-f770-52e9-8ba0-da97bd2d6088" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{258}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510745} :answer-field/id #uuid "af481ffe-765a-562a-87bb-3829101afbf0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510740}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2014768a-a233-5eee-b9c5-afc2675420b0" :question/problem "![](images/cc/ccdb5bb029197875aaeebfc899dff4d25597b657e64ea95d1b34d09df0a37789.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{7},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $tan⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/af/af413350ef3c9edde0eebf49cb8f37c8b20791f4bb01e04adaacaa0c378d28a1.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{7})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{43}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{6}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{7}.$\n\nKnowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{7})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{7})}=\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{\\sqrt{6}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{\\sqrt{6}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{43\\cdot 6}}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{258}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510730 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1909811c-da8d-539b-aa89-cdc5b420a83f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510725} {:answer/id #uuid "2fc3517e-1300-5538-b247-d948b579f7f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510729} {:answer/id #uuid "ec9189a3-d0b3-5483-9c84-198750079c85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{58}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510748} {:answer/id #uuid "f3b2d445-8bcf-5fd5-a74d-dc7c7c42cc23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{14}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510750} {:answer/id #uuid "f7e48c42-ff53-5bcc-b008-e6ccbde37ace" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{19}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510752}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3b2d445-8bcf-5fd5-a74d-dc7c7c42cc23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{14}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510750} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5c54992-c621-521f-8066-259a4e4e6c43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510742}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3c08d8db-de56-5ccc-8319-0573cd7cc4f0" :question/problem "![](images/d0/d04fe72eb2ad53b69837aaf402addd75d9b087225d70e876233e23092fc85764.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{5},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $tan⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/51/51778a4029b91dbbd51014a8f4a3e7e1fc772efe96058f41b21bbf408f113cce.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{18}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{7}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5}.$\n\nKnowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{5})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5})}=\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{\\sqrt{7}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{\\sqrt{7}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{\\sqrt{7}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{7}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{18\\cdot 7}}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{126}}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510733 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19cd8750-e5e7-52ce-b115-cbd111d3d902" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{390}}{39}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510726} {:answer/id #uuid "5ba5953e-1bc3-52b3-949b-b1a3372ec514" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{390}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510734} {:answer/id #uuid "dca87888-b247-5218-bd97-b52279791479" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510746} {:answer/id #uuid "ed349bbe-0002-555d-b774-e4baa909f28c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{79}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510749} {:answer/id #uuid "f51c1080-9d67-5d5c-9ad2-f5bee4c4e810" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510751}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ba5953e-1bc3-52b3-949b-b1a3372ec514" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{390}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510734} :answer-field/id #uuid "89a72e76-dfbf-51a2-96d4-d8c440244cee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510736}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "55b50355-dd9b-57b2-ba6b-ec0a86439c05" :question/problem "![](images/f2/f2c66fc4d5fa2ac5b2d6263764cf1eaa4e845680561833c5bfa46743df3408d2.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{7},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $tan⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/af/af1047be250d877d836851da681e583ada8f01d2022e5c9671aa83173ed23fa3.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{7})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{39}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{10}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{7}.$\n\nKnowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{7})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{7})}=\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{\\sqrt{10}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{\\sqrt{10}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{39\\cdot 10}}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{390}}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510744 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bc4984a-76a7-5e40-bec6-4baeb697eb93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510732} {:answer/id #uuid "93b2d161-28c4-50ff-962d-90ee2fbdac17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510737} {:answer/id #uuid "a332604f-d236-5a81-8925-b96c1993b9c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510739} {:answer/id #uuid "b382a9d0-fd0f-530d-8c12-c535c8651899" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510741} {:answer/id #uuid "c8cc011f-2ad6-5dc2-af67-11ebde52edbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510743}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93b2d161-28c4-50ff-962d-90ee2fbdac17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510737} :answer-field/id #uuid "68af0948-d377-54ba-bc95-3bf16b5f82fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510735}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cab2c352-408a-5716-9c82-d09e82069fc3" :question/problem "![](images/dd/dd9061a8ef1b2f0d8fe9d730f18c5e10bef42a9acd33f23d36efb3728ce0614b.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{15}{17},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $tan⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/ba/bad705982f073623644ed540e26cfad849b2dd078c5c64772a0be3937d618a27.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{15}{17}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{15}{17}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{15}{17}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{15}{17})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{225}{289}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{64}{289}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{8}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{8}{17}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{15}{17})}{(\\frac{8}{17})}=\\frac{15}{8}.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent or Cotangent Using the Pythagorean Identity"}]}
